The function under test is `open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel`. Its documented rule says: “The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.” The “no relaxation” part means it calls `_span_occurs` with an empty relaxations tuple, and `_span_occurs`’s own documented rule is: “Return None when the span is not in the document, else how it was matched (‘verbatim’ or a relaxation).”

The two reply texts are, in full, `{"value": "x", "span": "a  b", "document": "a b"}` and `{"value": "x", "span": "a b", "document": "a b"}`. For the first reply, the span `"a  b"` is not literally present in the document `"a b"`, so the rule requires `_span_occurs` to return `None`, and therefore `_grounding_kernel` must return `SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT`. For the second reply, the span `"a b"` is present in the document, but the value `"x"` is not contained in that span, so the rule requires `_grounding_kernel` to return `VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN`. The implementation, however, normalizes whitespace in both span and document before checking occurrence, so for the first reply it finds the normalized span `"a b"` inside the normalized document `"a b"` and proceeds to the value check, returning `VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN` for both replies. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart.

What would make me wrong is the observation that `_grounding_kernel` returns `SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT` for the first reply text, or equivalently that `_span_occurs` returns `None` for the span `"a  b"` against the document `"a b"` with no relaxations.